Rebuild the -mini variable table from the template's environment, not the defaults.

Two caches in the mini GUI hold the plugin's configs.yaml defaults and are never
refreshed when a template points the environment somewhere else:

1. BadgerVariableTable keeps its own copy of the environment configs and
   re-instantiates the environment from them in refresh_current_values().  That
   copy is only ever set in select_env(), from whatever is in the parameter
   editor at the time -- i.e. the defaults.  The table therefore queries the
   wrong machine: every refresh re-loads the default one, and variables only the
   template's machine has come back as errors.

2. routine_page.vars_env -- the table's rows -- is built in select_env() from
   configs["variables"], which factory.load_plugin computed once from an
   environment instantiated with those same defaults.  So even with (1) fixed,
   the table lists the default machine's variables and none of the template's.

Fix: re-run add_var() right after the template's params reach the editor, drop
the stale env instance, and rebuild vars_env from an environment carrying the
template's params -- the way factory.load_plugin builds the cached list.  If
that environment cannot be built, the cached list is kept and the failure is
logged, so a template on the default machine behaves exactly as before.

--- a/badger/gui/mini/pages/routine_page.py
+++ b/badger/gui/mini/pages/routine_page.py
@@ -406,6 +406,30 @@
             # to load the new environment
             self.env_box.set_selected_env_name(env_name)
             self.env_box.edit_env_params.set_params_from_dict(env_params)
+            # Both the variable table's env configs and the variable list
+            # itself were captured from the plugin defaults (in select_env and
+            # in factory.load_plugin respectively).  Now that the template's
+            # params are in the editor, rebuild both from them.
+            env_class, env_configs = self.add_var()
+            self.env_box.var_table.env_class = env_class
+            self.env_box.var_table.configs = env_configs
+            self.env_box.var_table.env = None
+            try:
+                env = instantiate_env(env_class, env_configs)
+                # Read the class attribute after instantiation: an environment
+                # that deduces its variables repopulates it on construction.
+                var_names = env_class.variables
+                var_bounds = env.get_bounds(var_names)
+                self.vars_env = [
+                    {name: _round_bounds_inward(var_bounds[name])}
+                    for name in var_names
+                ]
+            except Exception:
+                logger.warning(
+                    "Could not read the variable list from the template's "
+                    "environment params; keeping the ones cached for the "
+                    f"plugin defaults:\n{traceback.format_exc()}"
+                )
 
         else:
             raise BadgerEnvNotFoundError(
